## The Two-Parameter Generic

`Map[K,V]` has TWO type parameters: `K` for keys and `V` for values. This gives you enormous flexibility — you can pair any key type with any value type.

### Common Map Variations

```xiom
let name_to_score: Map[Str,Int] = Map[Str,Int].new();
let id_to_name: Map[Int,Str] = Map[Int,Str].new();
let product_in_stock: Map[Str,Bool] = Map[Str,Bool].new();
let price_list: Map[Str,Float64] = Map[Str,Float64].new();
```

### Using Different Key-Value Combinations

```xiom
id_to_name.insert(1001, "Alice");
id_to_name.insert(1002, "Bob");

product_in_stock.insert("SKU-001", true);
product_in_stock.insert("SKU-002", false);

price_list.insert("apple", 0.99);
price_list.insert("banana", 0.59);
```

### Generic Functions on Any Map

```xiom
fn map_size[K,V](m: Map[K,V]) -> Int {
  return m.len();
}

fn key_exists[K,V](m: Map[K,V], key: K) -> Bool {
  return m.contains(key);
}
```

### Full Program

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let ids: Map[Int,Str] = Map[Int,Str].new();
  ids.insert(100, "Alice");
  ids.insert(200, "Bob");

  let stock: Map[Str,Bool] = Map[Str,Bool].new();
  stock.insert("potion", true);
  stock.insert("elixir", false);

  match ids.get(100) {
    Some(name) -> io.println("ID 100: " + name);
    None -> io.println("Not found");
  }

  match stock.get("potion") {
    Some(in_stock) -> io.println(in_stock);
    None -> io.println("Unknown item");
  }

  return 0;
}
```

